    "name":  "Testicular torsion",
    "module":  "male-genital",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Testicular torsion is a urological emergency caused by twisting of the spermatic cord, compromising testicular blood supply and causing ischaemia. It is most common in adolescents (peak incidence 12–18 years) and is associated with the \u0027bell-clapper\u0027 deformity. It presents with sudden severe unilateral scrotal pain, often with nausea and vomiting, and an elevated, transversely lying (\u0027horizontal\u0027) testicle with absent cremasteric reflex. Time to surgery is critical: salvage rates are \u003e90% within 6 hours, ~50% at 12 hours, and near-zero after 24 hours. Diagnosis is clinical — scrotal exploration must not be delayed for imaging if clinical suspicion is high.",
